An autoencoder is a type of artificial neural network used to learn efficient codings of unlabeled data ( unsupervised learning ). [1] [2] An autoencoder learns two functions: an encoding function that transforms the input data, and a decoding function that recreates the input data from the encoded representation. The autoencoder learns an efficient representation (encoding) for a set of data, typically for dimensionality reduction .

Variants exist, aiming to force the learned representations to assume useful properties. [3] Examples are regularized autoencoders ( Sparse , Denoising and Contractive ), which are effective in learning representations for subsequent classification tasks, [4] and Variational autoencoders, with applications as generative models . [5] Autoencoders are applied to many problems, including facial recognition , [6] feature detection, [7] anomaly detection and acquiring the meaning of words. [8] [9] Autoencoders are also generative models which can randomly generate new data that is similar to the input data (training data). [7]
Mathematical principles [ edit ]
Definition [ edit ]

An autoencoder is defined by the following components:

    Two sets: the space of decoded messages X {\displaystyle {\mathcal {X}}} {\displaystyle {\mathcal {X}}} ; the space of encoded messages Z {\displaystyle {\mathcal {Z}}} {\displaystyle {\mathcal {Z}}} . Almost always, both X {\displaystyle {\mathcal {X}}} {\displaystyle {\mathcal {X}}} and Z {\displaystyle {\mathcal {Z}}} {\displaystyle {\mathcal {Z}}} are Euclidean spaces, that is, X = R m , Z = R n {\displaystyle {\mathcal {X}}=\mathbb {R} ^{m},{\mathcal {Z}}=\mathbb {R} ^{n}} {\displaystyle {\mathcal {X}}=\mathbb {R} ^{m},{\mathcal {Z}}=\mathbb {R} ^{n}} for some m , n {\displaystyle m,n} {\displaystyle m,n} .

    Two parametrized families of functions: the encoder family E ϕ : X → Z {\displaystyle E_{\phi }:{\mathcal {X}}\rightarrow {\mathcal {Z}}} {\displaystyle E_{\phi }:{\mathcal {X}}\rightarrow {\mathcal {Z}}} , parametrized by ϕ {\displaystyle \phi } {\displaystyle \phi } ; the decoder family D θ : Z → X {\displaystyle D_{\theta }:{\mathcal {Z}}\rightarrow {\mathcal {X}}} {\displaystyle D_{\theta }:{\mathcal {Z}}\rightarrow {\mathcal {X}}} , parametrized by θ {\displaystyle \theta } {\displaystyle \theta } .

For any x ∈ X {\displaystyle x\in {\mathcal {X}}} {\displaystyle x\in {\mathcal {X}}} , we usually write z = E ϕ ( x ) {\displaystyle z=E_{\phi }(x)} {\displaystyle z=E_{\phi }(x)} , and refer to it as the code, the latent variable , latent representation, latent vector, etc. Conversely, for any z ∈ Z {\displaystyle z\in {\mathcal {Z}}} {\displaystyle z\in {\mathcal {Z}}} , we usually write x ′ = D θ ( z ) {\displaystyle x'=D_{\theta }(z)} {\displaystyle x'=D_{\theta }(z)} , and refer to it as the (decoded) message.

Usually, both the encoder and the decoder are defined as multilayer perceptrons . For example, a one-layer-MLP encoder E ϕ {\displaystyle E_{\phi }} {\displaystyle E_{\phi }} is:

    E ϕ ( x ) = σ ( W x + b ) {\displaystyle E_{\phi }(\mathbf {x} )=\sigma (Wx+b)} {\displaystyle E_{\phi }(\mathbf {x} )=\sigma (Wx+b)} 

where σ {\displaystyle \sigma } {\displaystyle \sigma } is an element-wise activation function such as a sigmoid function or a rectified linear unit , W {\displaystyle W} {\displaystyle W} is a matrix called "weight", and b {\displaystyle b} {\displaystyle b} is a vector called "bias".
